WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:04.910
So today I'd like to talk about how we
find disease-causing mutations.

00:00:04.910 --> 00:00:08.750
This is going to rely on a computational
problem known as read mapping.

00:00:08.750 --> 00:00:12.370
And to get to that, I'd like to first
talk about the simple

00:00:12.370 --> 00:00:13.950
fact that the cost of sequencing a

00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:18.150
genome has decreased exponentially over
the past decade.

00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:21.950
According to NIH, in 2001, it cost about
$100,000,000

00:00:21.950 --> 00:00:23.340
to sequence a human genome

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:25.470
In 2013

00:00:25.470 --> 00:00:27.300
that's well below $10,000, and there's a

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:30.050
hope that we'll soon see
the $1,000 genome.

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:34.990
As genome sequencing has gotten cheaper,
biologists

00:00:34.990 --> 00:00:37.160
have moved from wanting to answer large

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:40.870
scale questions about species comparison
using genomes

00:00:40.870 --> 00:00:44.840
towards using personal genomes to answer
biological questions.

00:00:46.330 --> 00:00:50.240
So, we move from a generic average case
human being

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:53.870
to specific humans: me, Pavel, and Son,
and we want to

00:00:53.870 --> 00:00:58.040
say what is it that makes us unique as
humans individually.

00:00:59.630 --> 00:01:03.520
So if we look at our genomes, they are
largely similar, right?

00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:06.230
We only have small variations for the most
part.

00:01:06.230 --> 00:01:07.720
And the most common type of mutation,

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:10.490
causing these variations, is a
single nucleotide variation.

00:01:10.490 --> 00:01:13.110
So, we may see a substitution where in the

00:01:13.110 --> 00:01:16.010
same position we might see a C, an A, or a T.

00:01:16.010 --> 00:01:16.510
OK?

00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:20.540
So, the question then would be why do we
care about this?

00:01:20.540 --> 00:01:23.350
And there's many different reasons why we
want to do personal genomics.

00:01:23.350 --> 00:01:26.640
Probably, the most popular one is
that they

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:29.530
can help us with identifying the basis of
genetic diseases.

00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:36.380
Son and Pavel mentioned in the assembly
talk, that in 2010 this

00:01:36.380 --> 00:01:41.150
child Nicholas Volker became the first
human being to have his life

00:01:41.150 --> 00:01:43.650
saved because of genome sequencing.

00:01:43.650 --> 00:01:45.500
They were able to use gene therapy

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:48.650
after having performed a number of
unsuccessful surgeries.

00:01:49.860 --> 00:01:51.960
And so the hope is that this is going to
be the future of medicine.

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:56.450
That we'll be able to identify genetic
issues, using

00:01:56.450 --> 00:02:00.700
an individual genome, and use these to
design effective treatments.

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:07.290
Again, as the cost of sequencing a genome
has decreased, we're

00:02:07.290 --> 00:02:10.370
now seeing large scale projects to
sequence huge numbers of genomes.

00:02:10.370 --> 00:02:13.640
So in the UK there's a personal genome
project with a

00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:19.570
goal that's recently launched with a goal
of sequencing 100,000 human genomes.

00:02:19.570 --> 00:02:22.410
Of course, it's not a central focus of
what we want to talk about

00:02:22.410 --> 00:02:24.620
тoday we want to talk about a
computational problem today

00:02:24.620 --> 00:02:29.070
but when we start talking about a huge

00:02:29.070 --> 00:02:31.300
number of genomes like this, there are a
number of ethical issues.

00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:34.720
So for example, what if you had your
genome sequenced and you found out

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:39.260
that you had a high probability of getting
some genetic disorder later in life.

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:42.860
Would your employer be able to fire you
because

00:02:42.860 --> 00:02:44.480
of that, because they wouldn't want to
keep you around?

00:02:45.630 --> 00:02:46.550
And in the US we actually

00:02:46.550 --> 00:02:50.500
have had Congress pass the Genetic
Nondiscrimination Act

00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:54.450
of 2008, which said that employers are
not able to do this, right?

00:02:54.450 --> 00:02:57.490
If they find out genetic information,
they're not able to prevent

00:02:57.490 --> 00:03:01.060
you from getting health insurance or fire
you without a good reason.

00:03:03.110 --> 00:03:06.940
So, we want to move again as I said
towards a computational problem.

00:03:06.940 --> 00:03:10.500
And the computational problem is going to
work as follows.

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:15.000
Once we've sequenced a human genome we can
now have a reference genome.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:18.620
So we have a genome in a database that we
can compare against

00:03:18.620 --> 00:03:21.510
since we know that all human genomes are
very similar.

00:03:22.510 --> 00:03:24.410
So our question is, how is it that

00:03:24.410 --> 00:03:28.170
we can assemble individual genomes now
efficiently, using the

00:03:28.170 --> 00:03:30.220
reference genome information that we
already have?

00:03:32.900 --> 00:03:35.350
The question would be then, why don't we
use assembly, right?

00:03:35.350 --> 00:03:37.590
We've learned a lot about algorithms in
this course.

00:03:37.590 --> 00:03:39.810
We have a huge arsenal of different
methods

00:03:39.810 --> 00:03:42.872
that we can use, to answer questions on
bioinformatics.

00:03:42.872 --> 00:03:46.330
So, maybe the most obvious, solution that
we would do would

00:03:46.330 --> 00:03:50.630
be to say let's just sequence let's
assemble the individual genome

00:03:50.630 --> 00:03:53.990
from scratch, and then once we have
assembled back using the

00:03:53.990 --> 00:03:57.970
assembly methods that we already know, we
can compare it against the

00:03:57.970 --> 00:04:01.080
reference genome, and identify places
where the variance occur.

00:04:03.140 --> 00:04:07.500
That's a perfectly reasonable way of
looking at the problem, but traditionally

00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:11.030
constructing a de Bruijn graph or to
assemble a genome from

00:04:11.030 --> 00:04:14.540
scratch, that's something that takes a lot
of memory, our hope is

00:04:14.540 --> 00:04:17.490
that we can use the information that's
stored in the reference genome.

00:04:17.490 --> 00:04:20.220
If we have, if each individual

00:04:20.220 --> 00:04:24.400
has 99% similarity with the reference
genome

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:27.250
then we shouldn't need to start from
scratch.

00:04:27.250 --> 00:04:28.210
We should be able to use the

00:04:28.210 --> 00:04:30.240
information that's in that reference

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:33.680
to help us assemble each new individual
genome.

00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:38.420
So our end hope would be, maybe you could
go into a clinic and have your

00:04:38.420 --> 00:04:40.470
genome sequenced in 10 minutes using the
reference

00:04:40.470 --> 00:04:42.200
that it would collect and map your reads.

00:04:42.200 --> 00:04:45.106
Now what do I mean by mapping reads?

00:04:45.106 --> 00:04:48.487
By read mapping we mean that we want to
determine where each

00:04:48.487 --> 00:04:53.524
read, so each read that's generated by the
sequencing machine, has high similarity

00:04:53.524 --> 00:04:54.951
to the reference gene.

00:04:54.951 --> 00:04:56.979
OK, so rather than assembling from
scratch,

00:04:56.979 --> 00:04:58.903
we're going to take each of your
sequencing

00:04:58.903 --> 00:05:00.619
reads, and we're going to say where is

00:05:00.619 --> 00:05:02.825
this read most similar to the reference
genome?

00:05:02.825 --> 00:05:05.155
And, is there any variation there?

00:05:05.155 --> 00:05:08.489
So we might see a perfect
sub-string match here.

00:05:08.489 --> 00:05:14.165
We might see a substitution here, and we
may see a deletion there.

00:05:14.165 --> 00:05:16.273
We're going to focus on a simpler question

00:05:16.273 --> 00:05:18.691
though, which is going to help us
generalize the

00:05:18.691 --> 00:05:23.580
problem, which is where is it that the
sequencing reads from your genome,

00:05:23.580 --> 00:05:26.010
where is it that they match the reference
genome exactly?

00:05:26.010 --> 00:05:29.420
If we can answer this question, then maybe
we can generalize it.

00:05:29.420 --> 00:05:30.070
OK?

00:05:30.070 --> 00:05:32.300
And so, if we're given a single read,

00:05:33.450 --> 00:05:37.130
then this corresponds to a problem that
we've already

00:05:37.130 --> 00:05:38.810
seen back at the start of the class, which

00:05:38.810 --> 00:05:41.330
was the pattern matching problem for a
single pattern.

00:05:41.330 --> 00:05:44.130
So, you're given a string pattern, that
represents a read.

00:05:44.130 --> 00:05:45.880
And then a larger string genome.

00:05:45.880 --> 00:05:48.280
And you want to figure out which
positions

00:05:48.280 --> 00:05:52.680
that pattern matches the genome as a
sub-string.

00:05:52.680 --> 00:05:54.560
So, all we're doing then,

00:05:54.560 --> 00:05:57.290
is generalizing this to multiple reads, on
multiple patterns.

00:05:57.290 --> 00:06:01.310
We're finding, given a collection of
patterns, where is

00:06:01.310 --> 00:06:04.950
that those patterns match exactly in the
larger genome?

00:06:08.300 --> 00:06:11.510
We, you probably implemented a brute force
approach to the pattern

00:06:11.510 --> 00:06:14.340
matching problem in chapter 1 if you
solved the code challenge there.

00:06:15.820 --> 00:06:21.670
And probably the way that you did it was
to slide the pattern down the genome.

00:06:21.670 --> 00:06:24.160
And so if you had a pattern nana and you
were looking in the

00:06:24.160 --> 00:06:26.500
genome panamabananas, now we're going to
use

00:06:26.500 --> 00:06:29.120
words or things that are close to words.

00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:31.310
So we're going to use like, the structure
of English, so that

00:06:31.310 --> 00:06:33.180
I don't have to say a, c, g, t, t, g, g,

00:06:33.180 --> 00:06:35.480
g because this is a string problem.
OK?

00:06:35.480 --> 00:06:37.920
So we're going to assume that genome is
panamabananas, and

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:40.160
this is an example that will come back to.

00:06:40.160 --> 00:06:43.340
And so, say that we have this hypothetical
read nana.

00:06:43.340 --> 00:06:46.210
If we want to find the exact matches, then
we simply slide it down.

00:06:46.210 --> 00:06:50.410
We see in the first position, p and n
don't match, so we slide it down,

00:06:50.410 --> 00:06:55.030
a and n don't match, and then in the third
position we see a match of n.

00:06:55.030 --> 00:06:55.400
OK?

00:06:55.400 --> 00:06:58.140
And so, we say, well let's move to the
second position,

00:06:58.140 --> 00:06:59.790
we see two matches.

00:06:59.790 --> 00:07:01.630
The third position we don't match and so
we

00:07:01.630 --> 00:07:05.580
can slide the pattern down to the
next position.

00:07:05.580 --> 00:07:07.850
No match, no match, no match, no match.

00:07:07.850 --> 00:07:10.320
And then we finally reach an n again, and

00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:11.850
we see that there is a pattern match
there.

00:07:12.880 --> 00:07:14.760
And then we're at the end of the string.

00:07:14.760 --> 00:07:18.420
So, this seems like a perfectly reasonable
approach.

00:07:18.420 --> 00:07:21.410
We take each pattern and we just slide it
down the genome.

00:07:21.410 --> 00:07:23.480
And find out where the pattern matches.

00:07:24.700 --> 00:07:27.690
So I think maybe we can call it a day
early today.

00:07:27.690 --> 00:07:29.915
Or at least on this problem maybe we can
start

00:07:29.915 --> 00:07:32.880
going ahead and generalizing from
the exact pattern matching problem.

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:34.250
But I think we've got a perfectly

00:07:34.250 --> 00:07:37.050
reasonable way of solving a multiple
matching problem.

